# System settings and tools

System settings and diagnostic tools can affect more than the screen in front of someone. Identify what a change affects, who can make it and how its outcome will be known. Keep a diagnostic experiment separate from a command sent to equipment.

This reference pattern covers the system and tools routes registered by frontend **20261028.5**, used by Core **2026.11.1**. The [interface coverage register](interface-coverage.html) lists each entry. A route mapping establishes design ownership; it does not establish native visual compatibility or authorise a test against a real home.

<h2 id="system-capabilities">Choose the contract for the task</h2>

| Task | Required distinction | Related pattern |
| --- | --- | --- |
| State or template experiment | A displayed state or template result versus a physical device outcome | [Entity state](entity-state.html) |
| Action or event tool | A requested operation and its target versus a reported result | [Action results](action-results.html) |
| Logs and diagnostics | Recorded events and timestamps versus a proven cause | [History and statistics](history-and-statistics.html) |
| General settings | Instance-wide settings versus a person's profile preferences | [Locale, units and time](locale-units-and-time.html) |
| Analytics, AI and Labs | The specific enabled capability, data flow, account scope and known limitation | [Accounts and permissions](accounts-and-permissions.html) |
| Storage and hardware | Reported capacity or configuration versus a guarantee of recovery or reliability | [Backups](backup-and-restore.html) |
| Updates and Repairs | Availability, request progress and confirmed completion | [Updates and Repairs](updates-and-repairs.html) |

<h2 id="system-experiments">Explain what a diagnostic experiment changes</h2>

Keep technical labels and literal values intact. A state override changes Home Assistant's representation; it does not by itself command equipment to adopt that state. State changes and events may still affect automations. Explain that distinction before an action where it is relevant. A template preview is a calculation, not proof that its inputs are current or that a later action will succeed.

The current native entry point is **Settings > Tools**. Older instructions may call this **Developer tools**. Preserve the current interface label when giving a route.

| Tool | Design requirement |
| --- | --- |
| YAML | Distinguish check, reload and restart; name the scope and preserve native confirmation |
| States | Explain represented-state changes and their possible automation effects |
| Actions | Identify the actual action and resolved targets before execution |
| Action history | Separate recorded action data from a new request to repeat it |
| Template | Preserve literal syntax, selection and errors; a preview result is a calculation |
| Events | Distinguish listening from firing an event and explain possible automation effects |
| Statistics | Keep correction or deletion separate from read-only inspection, with units and time boundaries |
| Assist | Distinguish sentence interpretation from an executed action and preserve provider context |
| Debug and optional debug console | Retain native availability and permissions; distinguish observation from code execution |

The legacy `service` tool route redirects to `action` in the reviewed source. It is an alias, not an additional action interface.

An action tool can send real commands. Show its exact action, target, parameters and available permission checks, and keep its result separate from a subsequent device report. Do not label a live command “Preview”. Examples in this guide never invoke these tools.

