<h2 id="system-settings">Make settings scope and data use clear</h2>

Name whether a setting affects the current browser, the signed-in account or the whole instance. Use the actual native choice and its current value. Explain a changed prerequisite, unavailable feature or administrator restriction without hiding the setting's meaning.

For analytics, AI and Labs, describe the specific feature and relevant data destination from its actual configuration. Do not invent a universal “private”, “local only” or “AI off” guarantee. Enabling a provider, exposing an entity to Assist and enabling an experimental interface feature are different choices. Keep the native notices, consent steps and rollback information where supplied.

<%= example('system-privacy-choice') %>

<h2 id="system-diagnostics">Show observations with their limits</h2>

Label a log's timestamp and level without assuming that the nearest event caused an outage. An old retained value must not look like a new diagnostic response. Provide redaction guidance before copying logs; never encourage a person to publish credentials, private location history or full household data as part of a routine issue report.

Storage needs the volume or location, unit, total/used/available interpretation and observation time. Keep decimal GB and binary GiB distinct. Do not infer a recovery guarantee from free space or a backup file count. Hardware identifiers and connectivity values should preserve their literal format with useful labels and an accessible text alternative to topology diagrams.

<%= example('system-storage-status') %>

<h2 id="system-presentation">Use the shared components</h2>

Use [tables](../components/table.html) for comparable observations, [summary lists](../components/summary-list.html) for named attributes and [details](../components/details.html) for secondary technical explanation. Technical content uses code styling only for literal syntax. It still needs the shared heading scale, quiet borders, visible focus and readable body text.

Keep input boundaries distinct from card dividers. Where a native editor needs a specialist code control, preserve line navigation, accessible naming, selection, undo and validation. Do not simulate a rich editor with a decorative text box or replace actionable diagnostics with coloured status dots. State loading, no results, permission denied, stale data and failed retrieval explicitly.

<h2 id="system-acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

1. Identify the exact tool and account permission before testing. Use a disposable fixture for state/event experiments; do not use a household device as an accessibility fixture.
2. Check that Save, Reload, Restart, Run and a preview label describe different actual operations. Test failures and unconfirmed responses without discarding a useful draft.
3. Verify instance, account and browser scope. Test unsupported installation options and features that disappear when their integration is unloaded.
4. Check units, timestamps, redaction, empty and stale records. Preserve literal identifiers in copy operations.
5. Use keyboard-only navigation, 320px width, enlarged text and forced colours. Verify errors are connected to their controls and that dialogs restore focus.

The examples provide local, invented demonstrations. See [native task accessibility](native-task-accessibility.html) and [compatibility](compatibility-register.html) for the separate evidence needed before making a live implementation claim.

